From fa0b51680d00749b00d3a570300ecb7355d891ed Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 23:01:43 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/3] fix(parser): raise on table shape mismatch instead of
 misaligning silently

parse_table mapped cells to columns positionally via dict(zip(...)). A row
of the wrong width therefore shifted every field after the discrepancy and
returned without complaint. From the state audit, one extra leading cell:

  expected  {'rank': '1', 'team_name': 'Cue Crew', 'wins': '10',       ...}
  actual    {'rank': '',  'team_name': '1',        'wins': 'Cue Crew', ...}

Downstream that does not surface either: _to_int('Cue Crew') returns None
(database/ingest.py:195), so the row lands in StandingsSnapshot looking
ordinary, with losses holding the value that belonged to wins.

parse_table now validates each row's width against the declared columns and
raises TableShapeError naming the selector, the row index, the declared
columns and the cells actually received. strict=False keeps the old
skip-and-continue behaviour for exploratory work.

Two related cases handled while here:

  - Header rows inside <tbody> (every cell a <th>) are skipped rather than
    returned as a data row whose values are the column captions.
  - A table selector matching nothing now raises TableNotFoundError in
    strict mode. Returning [] rendered "the markup moved" and "this league
    has no standings" identically.

Callers get strict mode by default, so a shape change stops the scrape
rather than feeding the database. That is the intent: a wrong number is
more expensive than a failed run, because it cannot be identified later.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01M6T5FzSsbYhfhhdUJxAjKz
