From edcc35ea26282cc320bdeee1c750ddb9e1139b72 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 23:01:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] refactor(parser): drop dead selector keys and correct the
 map's own claim

TEAM_PAGE["roster_table_selector" / "roster_row_selector" / "roster_columns"]
and PLAYER_PAGE["stats_columns" / "stats_table_selector"] were read by
nothing. Roster parsing uses MATCH_PAGE selectors (team_scraper.py:60,64,77);
player parsing used no map at all.

They were not merely unused, they were a trap. PLAYER_PAGE["stats_columns"]
declared [match_date, opponent, skill_level, points_earned, result] while
_parse_match_row reads [match_date, opponent, result, skill_level,
points_earned]. Positions 2, 3 and 4 disagree, so the obvious cleanup --
wiring the declared map into the parser -- would have silently filed skill
level as the match result.

The module docstring claimed every selector had been "updated with real
selectors from league.poolplayers.com portal inspection". That held for
MATCH_PAGE only; STANDINGS_PAGE, TEAM_PAGE and PLAYER_PAGE are still
generic guesses such as table.standings. The docstring now states
verification status per map, and records what was removed and why so the
keys are not reinstated casually.

Keys still in use (TEAM_PAGE schedule_*, all of MATCH_PAGE, STANDINGS_PAGE)
are untouched, and a test asserts they stay.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01M6T5FzSsbYhfhhdUJxAjKz
