From 90dbc7e559db188608681c2bac05d8f4da8335a6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 23:01:44 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] fix(scraper): identify match tables by their headers, not
 by cell count

_extract_match_history walked every <table> on the player page and treated
any row of three or more cells as a match. A navigation bar, a lifetime
summary widget and a header row all clear that bar. Against the audit
fixture -- one real match table plus two unrelated ones -- it returned five
matches, four of them invented:

  {'match_date': 'Home', 'opponent': 'Teams', 'result': 'Standings'}   nav bar
  {'match_date': 'Won',  'opponent': 'Played', 'result': 'PPM'}        widget header
  {'match_date': '42',   'opponent': '60',     'result': '2.1'}        widget data
  {'match_date': 'Date', 'opponent': 'Opponent', 'result': 'Result'}   table header

Those flow into PlayerMatch through ingest_player_matches
(database/ingest.py:169) and, because PlayerMatch is unique on
(player_id, match_date, opponent), each lands as a distinct row and inflates
every count derived from it.

A table now qualifies only if its headers name both a date column and an
opponent column. A table with no <th> does not qualify, deliberately:
skipping a real table produces no data and a log line, while admitting a
fake one produces wrong data that cannot be told from real matches later.
When tables exist but none qualify, that is logged at WARNING so "found
nothing" cannot be read as "played nothing".

Scope: this changes only which tables are scanned. _parse_match_row's field
mapping is byte-identical, and nothing here touches the Match model or the
ingestion path -- both remain open audit findings.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01M6T5FzSsbYhfhhdUJxAjKz
